lish a patching process. Avoid embedding secrets in images or source repositories.\n\nRun application processes as non-root users where possible. Drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, avoid privileged containers, restrict host mounts, and protect access to the Docker daemon socket. Apply network segmentation and expose only required ports.\n\nUse volumes or external storage for important data. Configure resource limits, health checks, logging, and monitoring. Keep development, CI, and production configuration separate. Promote the same tested image between environments rather than rebuilding different images for each deployment target.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Phases and Goals — Build Tool Fundamentals** is complete only when the expected Docker state and application behavior are verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The correct Docker context and host were selected.\n- The intended image, container, network, or volume was used.\n- Configuration values and runtime permissions are correct.\n- The expected container process is running.\n- Application logs do not show unresolved startup failures.\n- Required network paths and service endpoints work.\n- Persistent data remains available after container recreation where required.\n- Resource limits and security settings match the intended policy.\n- The image tag or digest can be traced to the validated build.\n- The operation can be repeated using documented steps.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a small application image using a Dockerfile. Build the image with a versioned tag and run it as a container with an explicitly published application port.\n\nInspect the image and container configuration. Read application logs and verify the service endpoint. Introduce one controlled problem, such as an incorrect port mapping or invalid startup command. Observe the failure and collect evidence using Docker inspection and logs.\n\nCorrect the configuration and recreate the container. Confirm that the application works and explain which configuration change resolved the issue. If the application requires persistent data, verify that the data remains available after replacing the container.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Phases and Goals — Build Tool Fundamentals** means, why Docker uses the relevant capability, which components participate, how the operation works, and how the result is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the initial state, the exact Docker configuration or commands, the resulting image or container behavior, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply listing commands. Explain what each command changes, which component performs the work, and how the final application behavior is confirmed."
